> 2. **Capital Stock Evolution**:
>    - The evolution of capital stock over time can be expressed mathematically as:
>      \[
>      K_t = p K_{t-1} + I_t
>      \]
>      where:
>      - \( K_t \): Capital stock at time \( t \)
>      - \( p \): Retention factor representing depreciation (1 - depreciation rate)
>      - \( K_{t-1} \): Capital stock at the previous time period
>      - \( I_t \): Investment inflows (changes) occurring during the period.
> 
> 3. **Investment Calculation**:
>    - Investment is determined by the product of the savings rate and GDP:
>      \[
>      I_t = \text{savings rate} \times \text{GDP}_{t-1}
>      \]
> 
> 4. **Forecasting Future Capital**:
>    - The capital accumulation process is projected over multiple years by iterating the above equations, allowing for changes in savings rates and depreciation to be factored into future estimations.
> 
> 5. **Impact of Previous Economic Changes**:
>    - The model incorporates a persistence factor representing impacts from previous years that continue to affect GDP. This is mathematically represented as:
>      \[
>      \text{Impact}_{t} = (1 - \omega) \cdot \text{Impact}_{t-1} - \text{log-change}_{t-1}
>      \]
>      where:
>      - \( \omega \): Persistence parameter (a measure of decay over time).
>      - \(\text{log-change}_{t-1}\): Economic impacts captured from the last time step.
